libevtx is a library to access the Windows XML Event Log (EVTX) format.

LTRData fork
============

This is LTRData's fork of libyal/libevtx:
https://github.com/libyal/libevtx

The repository retains the upstream library, tools and documentation, with
changes for native-library integration and Windows builds. Recent LTRData work
includes MSVC build fixes and DLL export configuration changes. The history also
contains upstream updates to record access, including channel names and handling
of records without a computer name.

For issues specific to this fork, use:
https://github.com/LTRData/libevtx/issues

Project information:

* Status: alpha
* Licence: LGPLv3+

See COPYING, COPYING.LESSER, AUTHORS and the individual source headers for
licensing and attribution.


Components and usage
--------------------

* libevtx: native C library for reading EVTX files, record metadata, XML record
  representations and recovered records where available.
* evtxinfo: displays information about an EVTX file.
* evtxexport: exports records as text or XML, with separate modes for allocated
  records, recovered records, or both.
* pyevtx: Python bindings, built when Python support is enabled.

For example, using the built command-line tools:

    evtxinfo System.evtx
    evtxexport -f xml -m items System.evtx
    evtxexport -f xml -m recovered System.evtx

The default export format is text and the default mode is "items"; "-m all"
includes both allocated and recovered records. Recovery depends on the data
remaining in the file.

Rendering event messages can require the corresponding message resource files
and Windows Registry hives. evtxexport provides -p for the resource search path,
-r for a directory containing SYSTEM and SOFTWARE hives, and -s/-S for explicit
hive files. Use "evtxinfo -h" and "evtxexport -h" for the complete current options.


Native API and Windows integration
----------------------------------

The public C API is described in include/libevtx.h.in. Record access includes:

* UTF-8 and UTF-16 channel-name getters and size queries:
  libevtx_record_get_utf8_channel_name() and its _size counterpart;
  libevtx_record_get_utf16_channel_name() and its _size counterpart.
* UTF-8 and UTF-16 computer-name getters and size queries. A missing name is
  reported as unavailable (return value 0), rather than necessarily an error.
* XML string access and separate file APIs for allocated and recovered records.

For optional string values, check the return value before allocating or reading
the result. The documented convention is 1 for success, 0 when unavailable and
-1 on error; string-size queries include the terminating null character.

Windows DLL export settings are configured in the MSVC projects and the shared
msvscpp/libevtx.props file, which now defines FULL_DLL_EXPORT. Use matching
headers and binaries from the fork when consuming its native exports.

Multi-threading support was listed as planned in the original README. Although
the build configuration has threading-library and multi-threading options,
those options do not establish thread-safety for shared file or record handles.
Serialize access to a shared handle rather than assuming concurrent calls are
supported.


Building and testing
--------------------

The checked-in configurations describe the dependencies and build/test sequences:
https://github.com/LTRData/libevtx/tree/main/.github/workflows
https://github.com/LTRData/libevtx/blob/main/appveyor.yml

For a Unix source checkout, install the compiler, Autotools and dependencies
listed for the relevant platform, then run from the repository root:

    tests/build.sh

This helper runs synclibs.sh --use-head, autogen.sh, configure and make. It
downloads the supporting libyal libraries from their development heads, so
dependency contents can change between builds. Configure arguments can be
passed to the helper; for Python bindings, install the Python development
headers and use:

    tests/build.sh --enable-python

For the fixture-based test suite:

    ./synctestdata.sh
    tests/runtests.sh

The GitHub workflows include Linux, FreeBSD and macOS configurations. The
AppVeyor configuration includes Windows builds. On Windows, its MSVC sequence
uses synclibs.ps1 and autogen.ps1, followed by build.ps1 and runtests.ps1.
build.ps1 supports selecting VisualStudioVersion, Configuration, Platform and
PythonPath; its default Visual Studio version is 2022. The tracked MSVC projects
have configuration-specific toolsets, so use the build script and configuration
appropriate to your installed tools. These configurations describe build routes,
not a claim that every listed combination has been verified for this checkout.


Upstream documentation
----------------------

* Project documentation: https://github.com/libyal/libevtx/wiki/Home
* How to build from source: https://github.com/libyal/libevtx/wiki/Building

The documentation/ directory contains format notes, and manuals/ contains the
tool man pages. The upstream links remain the reference for the general libevtx
project; the fork-specific integration context is described above.
